    "scopeBoundary": "Owns accounting classification and recognition of launch transactions, with a clear books-versus-US-tax boundary; does not own estimating launch funding or forming an LLC.",
    "opening": "Startup-cost accounting identifies what each launch transaction represents and when it enters the accounts. A payment before opening can be an expense, equipment, inventory, a prepayment or a refundable deposit. Keep that book classification separate from cash budgeting and US tax recovery rules; one startup-spending total does not answer all three questions.",
    "answerHtml": "<p>Startup-cost accounting identifies what each launch transaction represents and when it enters the accounts. A payment before opening can be an expense, equipment, inventory, a prepayment or a refundable deposit. Keep that book classification separate from cash budgeting and US tax recovery rules; one startup-spending total does not answer all three questions.</p>",
