    "id": "N080",
    "slug": "financial-reporting",
    "title": "Financial Reporting: Close, Reconcile and Review the Statements",
    "seoTitle": "Financial Reporting: A Clear Close Workflow",
    "description": "Build a financial-reporting workflow from reconciled records to a reviewed statement package. Follow a checked cash, equity and balance-sheet control example.",
    "metaDescription": "Build a financial-reporting workflow from reconciled records to a reviewed statement package. Follow a checked cash, equity and balance-sheet control example.",
    "primaryKeyword": "financial reporting",
    "scopeBoundary": "Owns the preparation, reconciliation, review and release of a financial-reporting package; not ratio interpretation, financial-statement analysis or the separate income-statement template.",
    "opening": "Financial reporting turns a defined set of business records into a consistent, reviewed information package. Set the entity, period and accounting basis; reconcile the ledger and supporting schedules; prepare connected statements and explanatory notes; then approve and retain a controlled version. The reporting workflow comes before analyzing ratios or using the figures in a decision.",
    "answerHtml": "<p>Financial reporting turns a defined set of business records into a consistent, reviewed information package. Set the entity, period and accounting basis; reconcile the ledger and supporting schedules; prepare connected statements and explanatory notes; then approve and retain a controlled version. The reporting workflow comes before analyzing ratios or using the figures in a decision.</p>",
    "answerTitle": "Quick answer",
    "tag": "Break-Even Concepts",
